{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-01",
 "criterion": "An operation whose result is already present and identical is recorded as already satisfied, and its effect is not repeated. For a registry version, stderr shows `warning: <package> <version> is already published; skipping`. During `release execute`, a Python release is satisfied only when every file its build produces is on the index, so a rerun after a partial upload uploads only the missing files. The check that finds a tagged package still to finish reads the version-level answer and does not build."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-02",
 "criterion": "After a run fails partway, rerunning the same command with no state file from the failed run performs only the missing operations and writes a receipt. A local annotated tag on the expected commit that is missing on the remote is pushed before the receipt is written, regardless of its annotation text. `Nothing to release.` is reported only when every operation of every package tagged on the current commit is observed already satisfied. A failure to read the local tag state other than the tag being missing stops the run with an error before any effect."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-03",
 "criterion": "Any of these fails with E167 and is never adopted: a local or remote tag on another commit, a lightweight tag, a GitHub release with a different prerelease flag, an asset of the same name with a different size or sha256, or two assets with the same name. A tag's identity is its target commit plus being annotated; annotation text is never compared.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-04",
 "criterion": "A receipt is written only when every operation ends `published` or `alreadySatisfied`, and otherwise the run exits 1 with no receipt file (E172 when operations remain incomplete). The receipt records `intentDigest`, the envelope (`orchestrationRevision`, `releaseSourceRevision`, intent and artifact-manifest digests), and each operation's outcome and provider evidence.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-05",
 "criterion": "`workflow_dispatch` with `release_source_sha` releases that commit using the current workflow run's commit as the orchestration revision. The SHA must be 40 lowercase hex characters, resolve to that same commit, lie on the triggering `main` commit's history, and equal the exact merge commit of one GitHub-verified PR merged into `main` from `callisto/version-packages` or `callisto/version-packages--<40-hex>` in the same repository. An invalid explicit source fails the candidate job without planning or updating the release PR. A valid recovery run skips `verify`, uses `recovery-checks`, and does not update the release PR before execution."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-06",
 "criterion": "A tag push that GitHub refuses because the tagged commit's workflows differ from every branch tip fails with E180, naming the tag and target commit.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-07",
 "criterion": "A local `callisto release` refuses with E320, before any effect, when the registry already holds a version it would release and the package's files differ from the commit that set that version, or no commit on the first-parent history sets it. The version is read from the package manifest or, for a Cargo package that inherits it, from the workspace manifest; files are compared by content, so a reverted change and a change made inside a merge commit count. The error names the version commit; checking it out and running `callisto release` again adopts the published version and tags that commit. A registry that cannot be read stops the release before any effect. A package whose manifest is not committed is not checked here; the clean-tree rule covers it. A package whose files match that commit, or whose version the registry does not yet hold, is unaffected.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-08",
 "criterion": "`callisto release` and `callisto release --dry-run` print a warning on stderr for each package's last tag that exists in the repository but not on `origin` (one `git ls-remote` for all of them), saying its release may not have finished. The warning does not change the plan or the exit status, and nothing is printed when there is no `origin`, no tag, or the remote cannot be read."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-09",
 "criterion": "On a main push or manual dispatch without an explicit source, the repository's release workflow builds Callisto and takes the first release from `callisto release pending` as the source; with none pending, it examines the triggering commit. It rejects a selected source that is not an ancestor of the triggering main commit or is not the exact merge commit of exactly one GitHub-verified managed PR, rather than moving on to a newer release PR. Nothing is recorded on the PR; the committed decision and provider observations remain authoritative."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-10",
 "criterion": "After `execute` succeeds, including receipt upload, the workflow dispatches the oldest unfinished release other than the one just executed, by full merge SHA; when none remains, it refreshes the managed release PR for changesets now on main. A failed execution leaves the release unfinished and selected again. A failed dispatch fails the drain job and does not refresh the release PR. The drain job receives no registry secrets."
}
{
 "id": "REL-RERUN-11",
 "criterion": "`callisto release pending` lists, oldest first, the merged release commits whose release did not finish, from the last `--limit` commits (default 10) on HEAD's first-parent line that wrote the committed decision file (`--decision`, default `.callisto/release-decision.json`). A release is finished when every package in that commit's decision that has a publish target has a tag for its version, and every such package released to GitHub has a published (not draft) release; one that cannot be read counts as unfinished. A package missing from the current workspace, or without a publish target, is skipped; with no forge repository configured the GitHub release check is skipped; a decision that cannot be decoded is an error. The scan stops at the newest finished release, since releases are sequential. With `--from <sha>` only that release commit and newer ones count, and a SHA that is not a release commit on the branch is an error. The command lists the commit, its packages and versions, and what is missing, in text or in JSON under `pending`; it publishes nothing and exits 0 when there are none."
}
{
 "id": "REL-GH-01",
 "criterion": "Every package with a publish target gets an annotated, unsigned tag named by its tag template, with message `Release <tag>`, on the source commit, pushed to the `origin` push URL."
}
{
 "id": "REL-GH-02",
 "criterion": "A package that targets `github-release` gets `gh release create <tag> --repo <owner/repo> --verify-tag --draft`, adding `--prerelease` for prerelease versions. After all its assets upload, the release is published with `gh release edit <tag> --draft=false`."
}
{
 "id": "REL-GH-03",
 "criterion": "Release notes are the body of the package CHANGELOG section for the version, passed with `--notes-file`. For a package in a fixed group, the entries that the group's other members recorded for the same version follow, each as ``- `<ecosystem/name>`: <entry>``; the package's own section is kept whole (headings and paragraphs included) except its group boilerplate lines, an entry is left out when its text is identical to one the notes already hold, and a member without a changelog section adds nothing. With no changelog configured, a missing file, or a missing or empty section, Callisto passes `--generate-notes` and prints `notes: using generated notes (<reason>) for <package>` to stderr. A changelog that exists but cannot be read (a permission error, a directory, bytes that are not UTF-8) fails release planning with E125 naming the file, because generated notes would silently replace what the release PR reviewed."
}
{
 "id": "REL-GH-04",
 "criterion": "Each `[[release.artifact]]` becomes one upload named `asset-name` on the product package's GitHub release, tagged with the product's own tag and prerelease flag regardless of the asset owner package's version. An artifact whose `package` is not in the release fails with E179, and a `product-package` that is missing or does not target `github-release` fails with E197. When an artifact's owner package is selected but the product package is not, Callisto warns on stderr, records a `product-artifact-owner-without-product` diagnostic (surfaced in `--format json`; see REL-CMD-04), and derives no slot for that asset.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-GH-05",
 "criterion": "`release artifact-manifest` requires, for each slot, a regular non-symlink file named `asset-name` directly in `--artifact-dir` (`E221` for a symlink or non-file, an I/O error when missing) and records its sha256 and byte length. With slots, or with a manifest that seals Python files, `release execute` requires both `--artifact-manifest` and `--artifact-dir` (`E233`); otherwise it rejects either flag (`E232`). `--seal-python` records the wheel and sdist of every Python publish of the plan, sealed all or none, each named for its package and version, and `release execute` verifies each file's sha256 and byte length (`E139`) before any effect.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-GH-06",
 "criterion": "Before any effect, `release execute` fails with E139 when an artifact's bytes differ from the manifest. It fails with E140 unless `gh attestation verify` passes with the configured `--repo`, `--signer-workflow <repo>/.github/workflows/callisto-release.yml`, `--signer-digest` set to the orchestration revision, `--source-digest` and `--deny-self-hosted-runners`.",
 "is_error_case": true
}
{
 "id": "REL-GH-07",
 "criterion": "In the release workflow, `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` reaches only the `execute` job's execute step. The workflow's default permissions are empty, and the plan and build jobs receive no registry credential."
}
{
 "id": "REL-GH-08",
 "criterion": "Callisto finds a GitHub release by tag, draft or published, with the GraphQL `release(tagName:)` lookup followed by `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/releases/{id}`; it never reads the release list. After its own release create, asset upload or publish, a release or asset still absent is looked up again with 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s and 32s waits before the run fails with E167."
}

